  <%# 88% rather than the full width, so an edge of the next lift is always showing. That
      is #206, and it is the answer both bits of research give.

      Nielsen Norman on mobile carousels: "The illusion of continuity, created by half
      images or text that look like they are continued beyond the vertical edge of the
      screen, is a strong carousel cue", and against what this screen was relying on --
      "Dots are generally weak signifiers: because they are small, people often don't
      notice them." Smashing Magazine puts the same thing as a rule: "Always indicate a
      slice of the upcoming slide", and "Never use progress dots as the only way for users
      to navigate the carousel."

      The dots and the "3 of 5" stay. They say how many there are and where you sit, which
      a cut edge cannot; what they were failing at was being the only thing saying a swipe
      exists at all. Same for the progress bar #212 taught to move -- these are three cues
      doing different jobs, and only this one is visible before you touch anything.

      Restored to the full width from 640px up along with everything else: the lifts are a
      stack there, and 88% of a column would just be a narrow column. %>
  <section id="lift-panel-<%= position %>" aria-labelledby="lift-<%= position + 1 %>"
           class="mt-6 w-[88%] shrink-0 snap-center px-1 sm:w-full sm:px-0">
    <h2 id="lift-<%= position + 1 %>" class="text-xl text-gray-900">
      <%= @exercises[lift.first[:exercise_id]]&.name %>
    </h2>
    <%# A swipe surface nobody knows is there is worse than the scroll it replaced, so each
        panel says where it sits. The dots say there are others and roughly where; the count
        says how many, which dots alone never do. Both are written per panel and are true as
        rendered, which is why neither needs a scroll listener to stay honest.

        Not on a session of one lift, where there is nowhere to swipe to and "1 of 1" is a
        line of furniture. Not from 640px up either, where the lifts are stacked and every
        one of them is visibly on the page already. %>
    <%- if session_lifts.length > 1 %>
      <div class="mt-1 flex items-center gap-2 sm:hidden">
        <div class="flex gap-1" aria-hidden="true">
          <%- session_lifts.each_index do |dot| %>
            <div class="h-1.5 w-1.5 rounded-full <%= dot == position ? 'bg-gray-500' : 'bg-gray-300' %>"></div>
          <%- end %>
        </div>
        <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-gray-500"><%= position + 1 %> of <%= session_lifts.length %></p>
      </div>
    <%- end %>

    <%# Small caps in gray-500 is this screen's label register -- Warmup, RPE and Plate
        math all wear it, so that a label is always recognisable as one and never as a
        value. It was gray-400, which is 2.54:1 on white; a line nobody can read at
        arm's length with a bar in their hands may as well not be on the screen. %>
    <%- unless lift.select { |set| set[:is_warmup] }.empty? %>
      <p class="mt-2 text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-gray-500">Warmup</p>
    <%- end %>
    <%# A warmup row and a working-set row are the same row. #207: they were text-lg in a
        rounded-lg with py-2 against text-2xl font-semibold in a rounded-xl with py-3, which
        made a ramp step and a working set read as two different kinds of object when they
        are one kind -- a set, with a load, a rep count and a Done button. Both are now
        text-xl font-medium in the same box, and what says which is which is the "Warmup"
        label above this list, in words, where it was already being said.

        This does not put back the fade #133 removed, and must not: the list carried
        opacity-75, which took the Done button on a warmup to 4.24:1 against 7.56:1 at full
        strength -- a washed-out control, which is what a disabled one looks like, on the
        button that is the first thing anybody taps in a session. Size was what replaced
        that fade; the label is what replaces the size. %>
    <ul>
      <%- lift.select { |set| set[:is_warmup] }.each do |set| %>
        <%== render('workouts/_set_row', locals: { set:, position: }) %>
      <%- end %>
    </ul>

    <ul class="mt-3">
      <%- lift.reject { |set| set[:is_warmup] }.each do |set| %>
        <%== render('workouts/_set_row', locals: { set:, position: }) %>
      <%- end %>
    </ul>

    <%# Swapping the whole lift onto another movement, which is #365's one tap. Before this
        it was one edit per set through the set editor -- three for three sets, six on a
        lift with a ramp -- and the cost was paid at the worst possible moment, standing at
        the rack with the dumbbells taken and the barbell free. Three sets went into the log
        as the wrong movement on 2026-09-01 because logging the wrong one was quicker.

        Only while something is still unlifted. A completed set records a movement that was
        performed and does not move (#364), so on a lift that is finished this control could
        do nothing but say so -- and a disabled-looking row on a screen read at arm's length
        is worse than a row that is simply not there. The count says how many will move, so
        a part-finished lift does not promise more than it will do.

        Not the _exercise_options partial the set forms use: that reads @exercises as an
        array and partitions it into library and own, while the session holds the same rows
        as a hash keyed by id, because every panel looks a movement up by the id on its
        sets. Reshaping one to suit the other would cost a query or a copy on the hot path
        of this screen for a menu most sessions never open.

        The target is #lift-panels rather than this panel: a swap can merge two panels into
        one, and every position after it shifts. %>
    <%# One more set of this movement. #496.

        The screenshot on that issue is the last lift of a session with both sets done and
        nowhere to put a third. "Add a set" has existed since the beginning on the workout
        record and the set list, which are two pages away from the one a lifter is holding --
        so deciding to do another set meant leaving the session, adding it, and coming back.

        A plain button rather than a form of fields, because the numbers are already on the
        screen: the new set copies the last one of this movement, and anything different is
        corrected with the revision box the panel already carries. One tap, then one edit if
        it was not the same, instead of five fields with chalk on.

        Always offered, unlike the swap above it, which hides once every set is lifted. A
        finished lift is exactly when a lifter decides to do one more, and #364's argument for
        hiding the swap -- that lifted sets record what happened and must not move -- says
        nothing about adding a new one beside them.

        Targets #lift-panels rather than this panel: the progress header counts every set in
        the session, and adding one changes the count and the estimate above it. %>
    <form action="/workouts/<%= @workout[:id] %>/session/add" method="post"
          hx-post="/workouts/<%= @workout[:id] %>/session/add"
          hx-target="#lift-panels" hx-swap="innerHTML" class="mt-2">
      <%== csrf_tag("/workouts/#{@workout[:id]}/session/add") %>
      <input type="hidden" name="exercise_id" value="<%= lift.first[:exercise_id] %>">
      <button type="submit"
              aria-label="Add another set of <%= @exercises[lift.first[:exercise_id]]&.name %>"
              class="<%= button_style %> min-h-11 w-full border border-gray-300 bg-white px-3 text-sm font-medium text-gray-700 hover:bg-gray-50">
        Add a set
      </button>
    </form>

    <%- unless unlifted(lift).empty? %>
      <details class="group mt-2">
        <summary class="flex min-h-11 cursor-pointer items-center text-xs text-gray-500"><%== render('_chevron') %>Lifted a different movement</summary>
        <form action="/workouts/<%= @workout[:id] %>/session/swap" method="post"
              hx-post="/workouts/<%= @workout[:id] %>/session/swap"
              hx-target="#lift-panels" hx-swap="innerHTML" class="mt-2 flex items-end gap-2">
          <%== csrf_tag("/workouts/#{@workout[:id]}/session/swap") %>
          <input type="hidden" name="from_exercise_id" value="<%= lift.first[:exercise_id] %>">
          <label class="flex-1 text-xs text-gray-500" for="swap-<%= position %>">
            Swap <%= unlifted(lift).length %> remaining set<%= 's' if unlifted(lift).length > 1 %> for
            <select id="swap-<%= position %>" name="exercise_id"
                    class="<%= field_style %> mt-1 block w-full py-2 text-base text-gray-900">
              <%- @exercises.each_value do |exercise| %>
                <option value="<%= exercise[:id] %>"><%= exercise[:name] %></option>
              <%- end %>
            </select>
          </label>
          <button type="submit"
                  class="<%= button_style %> border border-sky-800 bg-sky-800 px-4 py-2.5 text-sm font-semibold text-white hover:bg-sky-900">
            Swap
          </button>
        </form>
      </details>
    <%- end %>

    <%# No copy of the rating scale here since #277. It was the footer of every panel that
        asked for a rating, which is how it stayed near the buttons it explains; it is now
        one copy pinned to the foot of the screen, rendered from views/workouts/session.erb,
        which is nearer to every panel than a copy inside one panel ever was.

        The guard went with it and had to. "Does this panel ask for a rating" is the wrong
        question for page furniture -- a footer that appeared and vanished as you swiped
        between a squat and a plank would be a control moving under the thumb -- so the page
        asks whether any set in the session can be rated. Same predicate, one level up. %>
  </section>
